1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Next to water, it's the world's most popular drink.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Centuries ago, it fueled the Industrial Revolution.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
And today, commuters keep their cup holders locked and loaded with lattes.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Coffee is a comfort to millions and a necessity to many more.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Coffee houses have brewed creativity and inspiration

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
since the first cafe opened in Constantinople.

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
As America experiences a new coffee house explosion,

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Roasting machines process millions of pounds a week

9
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:33,000
to keep up with the cult of the bean.

10
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:37,000
Now, coffee on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The world currently consumes more than 100 million bags

12
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:56,000
of coffee like these every year.

13
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,640
At the Starbucks Roasting Facility in Kent, Washington,

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,640
bags of green coffee beans weighing 132 pounds each

15
00:01:03,641 --> 00:01:06,640
arrive continually from Central and South America,

16
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:08,960
Indonesia, Africa, and Yemen.

17
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:12,320
This plant cleans, roasts, and packages

18
00:01:12,321 --> 00:01:14,880
more than 60 million pounds of coffee every year.

19
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:20,100
In the 300,000-square-foot storage area,

20
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:23,040
up to 20 ship containers are unloaded every day,

21
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:26,260
and the burlap bags are piled 18 feet high.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,440
Behind us and around us is about 2 million pounds of green coffee.

23
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:31,580
That's unroasted coffee.

24
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,460
We'll roast that and package it in about 7 to 10 days.

25
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:41,580
There's enough coffee in this warehouse to supply 2,000 Starbucks stores for about a week.

26
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:45,340
This is one of three of the company's American roasting plants,

27
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:49,460
and it supplies the coffee for Starbucks stores in the northwestern U.S.

28
00:01:49,461 --> 00:01:50,161
and in Asia.

29
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:56,560
A computerized inventory system determines when it's time to call up a new batch for roasting.

30
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,620
Before entering the roaster, the beans go through a cleaning process.

31
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:07,800
We load the green coffee in through a grate here on the floor, and a series of pneumatic

32
00:02:07,801 --> 00:02:11,720
elevators like this one carries the coffee into what we call our green bean cleaning

33
00:02:11,721 --> 00:02:12,720
machine.

34
00:02:12,721 --> 00:02:19,020
That machine uses a series of vibrating shelves, magnets, and vacuums to separate any foreign

35
00:02:19,021 --> 00:02:20,520
matter from the coffee.

36
00:02:20,521 --> 00:02:25,460
So for instance, stones, small sticks, small bits of metal.

37
00:02:25,461 --> 00:02:29,560
The green beans travel through a system of aluminum pneumatic tubes to one of the huge

38
00:02:29,561 --> 00:02:34,060
roasting machines.

39
00:02:34,061 --> 00:02:37,000
The roaster operates like a large clothes dryer.

40
00:02:37,001 --> 00:02:43,380
As it rotates, hot air is forced into the chamber to heat the beans.

41
00:02:43,381 --> 00:02:48,000
The roasted beans are spilled out into a large circular cooling pan and slowly stirred by

42
00:02:48,001 --> 00:02:49,000
a rotating arm.

43
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:53,260
This is one of six coffee roasters we have here at the Kent Roasting Plant.

44
00:02:53,261 --> 00:02:56,620
Three roast coffees and batches from between 200 and 700 pounds.

45
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,140
The coffee beans are roasted for 12 to 15 minutes

46
00:03:00,141 --> 00:03:05,500
at temperatures that range between 350 and 450 degrees Fahrenheit.

47
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:10,380
We need a different roast for each different coffee that we serve.

48
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:13,100
For instance, what we have back here is espresso roast.

49
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:14,960
This is our third darkest coffee.

50
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:18,860
This is a single ground, a 2% extra hot, no whip, two-pump chocolate.

51
00:03:18,861 --> 00:03:26,600
At Seattle's Pike Place Market, the very first of more than 6,000 Starbucks stores in the U.S., is busier than ever.

52
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:27,840
Three, please.

53
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:40,140
With the registers are set up and the way that our bar is set up, we actually have to toss the cups and throw them from the register to the graces.

54
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:44,800
There's a specialty coffee explosion percolating throughout the world.

55
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:49,340
and it's been led by Starbucks and founder Howard Schultz.

56
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:53,560
Today there's over 9,000 Starbucks stores in 35 countries

57
00:03:53,561 --> 00:03:56,500
serving almost 40 million people a week,

58
00:03:56,580 --> 00:03:58,900
and we employ over 100,000 people.

59
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:03,040
Schultz got the idea for the Starbucks chain while on a visit to Italy.

60
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:06,680
There's 1,500 coffee bars in Milan alone,

61
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:10,600
and I went from coffee bar to coffee bar not only in Milan

62
00:04:10,601 --> 00:04:12,680
but in other places, Verona as well.

63
00:04:12,681 --> 00:04:20,460
What really struck me was the sense of humanity, human connection around the coffee and coffee experience.

64
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:25,240
Coffee is one of the great romantic beverages of our time.

65
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:30,820
It's filled with such unique history, and it's a beverage of truth.

66
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:35,760
To tell the story of coffee, you have to start with the bean.

67
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:39,820
But what we call coffee beans are actually coffee seeds.

68
00:04:39,821 --> 00:04:46,820
Coffee cherries usually contain two of these seeds, which after processing and roasting

69
00:04:46,821 --> 00:04:51,620
are ground to make coffee.

70
00:04:51,621 --> 00:04:56,280
The first recorded evidence of coffee cultivation comes from Ethiopia at the beginning of the

71
00:04:56,281 --> 00:05:01,440
10th century AD by some estimates and as early as the 6th century AD by others.

72
00:05:01,441 --> 00:05:06,560
Legend has it that a goat herd named Kaldi first discovered coffee's kick when he noticed

73
00:05:06,561 --> 00:05:08,380
his goats eating it.

74
00:05:08,381 --> 00:05:09,680
Apparently, the goats got jumpy.

75
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:13,820
Coffee is native to Ethiopia.

76
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:14,820
It grows wild.

77
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,380
And so it's very reasonable to assume that, yes,

78
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:19,940
in their grazing, the goats ate this.

79
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:22,560
And it's quite conceivable that, yes,

80
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:26,440
the caffeine within the coffee altered their behavior,

81
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,740
particularly if they ate a lot of these cherries.

82
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:32,600
The first known consumption of coffee

83
00:05:32,601 --> 00:05:35,360
was as a food by a tribe called the Aromos in Ethiopia.

84
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,320
And what they did is they would take the raw coffee

85
00:05:38,321 --> 00:05:42,220
beans and cooked them in fat and squeezed them into a ball about the size of a baseball,

86
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:46,115
which they would sort of gnaw on as a kind of snack food.

87
00:05:46,116 --> 00:05:47,940
And at some point, the beans

88
00:05:47,941 --> 00:05:52,713
got transferred across the Red Sea to Yemen, probably via the slave trade.

89
00:05:52,714 --> 00:05:53,680
The Oromos were

90
00:05:53,681 --> 00:05:54,960
probably eating it.

91
00:05:54,961 --> 00:06:00,080
Yemen in southern Arabia is less than 200 miles from Ethiopia, and it's here

92
00:06:00,081 --> 00:06:04,789
that coffee trees were first cultivated on plantations in the 15th century.

93
00:06:04,790 --> 00:06:05,490
From Yemen,

94
00:06:05,491 --> 00:06:07,720
coffee-drinking spread throughout the Arab world.

95
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:15,660
It's a drink of attention, of acuity, of precision, of discipline, of intellect.

96
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:21,680
And it always struck me as perfect that the Arab civilization gave us mathematics.

97
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:28,580
In part, one could almost imagine, because of this sort of attentiveness to detail,

98
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:31,400
that their main stimulant of choice gave them.

99
00:06:31,401 --> 00:06:36,400
According to legend, the Arabs refused to allow the export of fertile seeds,

100
00:06:36,401 --> 00:06:40,400
so they could hold on to their coffee and their culture.

101
00:06:40,401 --> 00:06:46,400
It's Islam that became the repository of all the knowledge of the ancient Greeks

102
00:06:46,401 --> 00:06:49,400
and of classical Rome during the dark ages of Europe.

103
00:06:49,401 --> 00:06:52,400
And so you have this sort of image of sitting around, you know,

104
00:06:52,401 --> 00:06:55,400
beneath the blue dome of a Saharan sky,

105
00:06:55,401 --> 00:06:59,400
sipping your incredibly concentrated dose of coffee

106
00:06:59,401 --> 00:07:02,560
and then letting the human intellect run wild.

107
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,380
Around 1550, the world's first known coffee houses opened in Constantinople,

108
00:07:09,140 --> 00:07:10,400
now Istanbul, Turkey.

109
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:17,960
Turkish coffee is a blend of water, sugar, and coffee.

110
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:20,980
It's made in a device called an ibrick,

111
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:27,200
and the water, the coffee, and the sugar are brought to a boil three times.

112
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:34,400
Now here we are at the second boil and the traditional third boil.

113
00:07:34,401 --> 00:07:39,400
There is the Turkish coffee ready to be enjoyed.

114
00:07:39,401 --> 00:07:45,400
In the 1600s, coffee and coffee houses spread to Western Europe and England.

115
00:07:45,401 --> 00:07:52,400
Up until that point in the 17th century, everybody in Europe drank alcohol.

116
00:07:52,401 --> 00:07:57,400
The entire continent of Europe was mildly besotted all the time.

117
00:07:57,401 --> 00:08:00,400
Why? Because if you lived in London or Paris,

118
00:08:00,401 --> 00:08:03,400
you couldn't drink the water of the Thames or the River Seine

119
00:08:03,401 --> 00:08:07,400
unless you wanted to get cholera, dysentery, or some other parasitic disease.

120
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:11,400
You would start a day with beer.

121
00:08:11,401 --> 00:08:14,400
At 10 o'clock, instead of a coffee break, you'd have a beer break.

122
00:08:14,401 --> 00:08:16,400
And then at lunch, you'd have more beer.

123
00:08:16,401 --> 00:08:20,400
And people were constantly commenting on this sort of haze everybody was in.

124
00:08:20,401 --> 00:08:25,400
And so what happened with the introduction of coffee is that that slowly replaced that.

125
00:08:25,401 --> 00:08:29,240
Work and coffee became inseparable in the modern industrial age.

126
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:32,880
And perhaps coffee made the age possible in the first place.

127
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:36,720
You simply could not afford to be drunk

128
00:08:36,721 --> 00:08:39,700
while you were working the heavy-duty industrial machinery

129
00:08:39,701 --> 00:08:44,660
that gave rise to modern society or the Industrial Revolution as we know it.

130
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:49,140
And so caffeine became part of the Industrial Revolution

131
00:08:49,141 --> 00:08:54,520
because it became the drug of choice of the Protestant ethics,

132
00:08:54,521 --> 00:08:58,720
of the kind of anti-sensual, workaholic world

133
00:08:58,721 --> 00:09:02,660
that gave rise to the kind of the austerity of the Victorian age.

134
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:07,160
Coffee made a big splash in a city where tea would later take over

135
00:09:07,161 --> 00:09:11,180
when the first coffee house opened in London in 1652.

136
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,080
By 1700, there were more than 2,000.

137
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:17,680
They were called penny universities

138
00:09:17,681 --> 00:09:20,620
because for a penny you could buy a cup of coffee

139
00:09:20,621 --> 00:09:24,400
and spend hours listening to enlightening conversations and debates.

140
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:31,520
The cafes became the center of intellectual discussion, sober intellectual discussion.

141
00:09:31,521 --> 00:09:36,960
One of the foundations of modern democracy, the ballot box, first appeared in London's

142
00:09:36,961 --> 00:09:43,400
Turks Head Coffeehouse, where customers could anonymously cast votes on political controversies.

143
00:09:43,401 --> 00:09:48,600
Some London coffeehouses became headquarters for powerful business organizations, including

144
00:09:48,601 --> 00:09:52,920
Lloyd's of London at Lloyd's Coffeehouse, the London Shipping Exchange at the Baltic

145
00:09:52,921 --> 00:09:56,920
coffee house and the East India Company at the Jerusalem Cafe.

146
00:09:58,920 --> 00:10:01,563
But it was not just a stimulant for commerce.

147
00:10:01,564 --> 00:10:04,440
If you kind of look through the filter of history

148
00:10:04,441 --> 00:10:09,240
at the later part of the 17th century, the early part of the 18th century,

149
00:10:09,241 --> 00:10:12,184
the entire era is wired on caffeine.

150
00:10:12,185 --> 00:10:16,600
You see it in the literature, in the kind of reflexive

151
00:10:16,601 --> 00:10:23,180
cynicism of Jonathan Swift, the author of Gulliver's Travels, or A Modest Proposal,

152
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:29,480
which he suggested a solution to the Irish problem being the feeding of Irish babies

153
00:10:29,481 --> 00:10:31,471
to the hungry people of the world.

154
00:10:31,472 --> 00:10:35,160
Totally tongue-in-cheek, but a kind of wired caffeine cynicism.

155
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:41,180
Eminent authors like Swift and Alexander Pope, and painters like William Hogarth,

156
00:10:41,181 --> 00:10:46,420
sipped their coffee at Will's Cafe, while Isaac Newton frequented the Grecian coffeehouse.

157
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:51,600
So each café became associated with poetry or science or business,

158
00:10:51,601 --> 00:10:56,600
and from that, idea of these cafés devoted to particular disciplines

159
00:10:56,601 --> 00:10:58,600
grew the idea of a newspaper with different sections,

160
00:10:58,601 --> 00:11:01,600
because people would go to the café, milk down the conversations,

161
00:11:01,601 --> 00:11:04,600
put them into these little sort of publications

162
00:11:04,601 --> 00:11:06,600
that became the Martyr newspaper,

163
00:11:06,601 --> 00:11:08,600
and that is where the Martyr newspaper was actually born,

164
00:11:08,601 --> 00:11:12,600
in the coffee houses of London in the 1600s to 1700s.

165
00:11:12,601 --> 00:11:14,600
Throughout the 1600s,

166
00:11:14,601 --> 00:11:18,800
In the 1600s, the world's coffee supply came almost entirely from Yemen.

167
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:25,260
But the Dutch, who dominated the world's shipping trade, got their hands on some coffee seedlings

168
00:11:25,261 --> 00:11:31,980
and opened plantations in Salon in 1658 and Java in 1699.

169
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:36,020
The first coffee house in Paris opened in 1672.

170
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:43,460
Louis XIV was a coffee lover, and in 1714 the Dutch presented him with his own coffee tree,

171
00:11:43,461 --> 00:11:44,880
which he cherished.

172
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:48,300
Nine years later, a French naval officer,

173
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:50,000
Gabriel Mathieu de Cure,

174
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:52,580
took a seedling from the royal tree

175
00:11:52,581 --> 00:11:54,100
to Martinique in the Caribbean.

176
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:56,860
Experts say most of the coffee now grown

177
00:11:56,861 --> 00:11:58,180
in Central and South America

178
00:11:58,181 --> 00:12:01,040
is descended from that single plant.

179
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:05,600
A few of those seedlings would find their way

180
00:12:05,601 --> 00:12:07,080
to the Kona district of Hawaii.

181
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:10,080
Coffee plants were first brought to Hawaii

182
00:12:10,081 --> 00:12:12,020
from Brazil in 1825

183
00:12:12,021 --> 00:12:14,720
by Chief Boki, the governor of Oahu,

184
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:17,160
on his way back from a voyage to London.

185
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:21,480
In 1828, seedlings were planted in Kona

186
00:12:21,481 --> 00:12:24,520
on the Big Island of Hawaii by Reverend Samuel Ruggles,

187
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:27,220
giving rise to Hawaii's coffee industry.

188
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:32,080
Many Japanese immigrants were among the coffee-growing pioneers in Kona.

189
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:37,380
Many of the Japanese immigrants and their children,

190
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:38,760
they had no other skills.

191
00:12:38,761 --> 00:12:40,200
All they knew is coffee.

192
00:12:40,201 --> 00:12:46,420
So they just stayed here, and we had bad times, and then we had good times.

193
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:51,260
Hawaii, with its tropical climate and mineral-rich volcanic soil,

194
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:53,820
is the only coffee-producing state in America.

195
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:59,040
And the Kona district now produces some of the world's most expensive coffee beans.

196
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:06,400
But for decades, expensive coffee beans would be the last thing American consumers were interested in.

197
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:10,080
They wanted low-priced coffee, and lots of it.

198
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:17,120
In an effort to keep costs down, some coffee brands would change their blends, but not necessarily for the better.

199
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:23,080
One coffee tree yields around one pound of roasted coffee per year.

200
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,720
That's about 4,000 beans.

201
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:34,300
This 100-year-old living history farm, operated by the Kona Historical Society,

202
00:13:36,660 --> 00:13:39,980
grows and processes coffee the old-fashioned way.

203
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:48,200
It was a technology that was developed by the Japanese first generation and second generation beginning in the 1920s.

204
00:13:48,820 --> 00:13:50,980
So this was a small family mill.

205
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:57,200
It was meant primarily to process the ripe coffee fruit into a dried product called parchment.

206
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:01,140
I remember using cans like this.

207
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:03,720
If you notice this, a can like this.

208
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:07,460
When you're a little kid, you learn to count first.

209
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:09,080
So I learned to count one to ten.

210
00:14:09,081 --> 00:14:13,360
Jeffrey Matsuyama grew up in this area, on a coffee farm like this one.

211
00:14:13,361 --> 00:14:16,682
You fill ten of these and you could go play the rest of the day.

212
00:14:16,683 --> 00:14:17,720
I remember that too.

213
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:21,180
Most of the kids over here, we were on a coffee schedule.

214
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:26,780
We got out of school around August because that's when the harvest started.

215
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:29,420
They needed the children to harvest the coffee.

216
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:35,560
After picking, coffee cherries were taken to the farm's curry bar, or processing shed.

217
00:14:35,561 --> 00:14:39,060
Here, the outer fruit of the coffee cherry was removed,

218
00:14:39,061 --> 00:14:41,560
using a device called a pulper.

219
00:14:41,561 --> 00:14:45,560
I'd like to show you how simply it was made.

220
00:14:45,561 --> 00:14:48,560
Middle piece punched out like this, okay?

221
00:14:48,561 --> 00:14:54,560
What this does is squeezes the bean just like this, okay?

222
00:14:54,561 --> 00:14:58,560
See how the seeds come out and the pulp falls out on one side,

223
00:14:58,561 --> 00:15:00,560
seeds on the other.

224
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:17,360
After pulping, the wet coffee seeds, what we know as beans, still have their inner covering, known as parchment.

225
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:29,300
We're putting wet parchment, we call this parchment, throw it inside there, it shakes it, and it gets all the moisture as drying itself off.

226
00:15:31,780 --> 00:15:34,780
All it does is actually drip dry the coffee.

227
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:38,560
But over here, we got one better.

228
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:46,560
We have a drying platform here, all right?

229
00:15:46,561 --> 00:15:51,560
We get the wet beans, drag it out like this.

230
00:15:51,561 --> 00:15:55,560
All the way, we start from one end of the platform.

231
00:15:55,561 --> 00:15:59,560
Keep spreading it out. Use a rake like this.

232
00:15:59,561 --> 00:16:02,560
No good for oak leaves, but watch what it does to coffee.

233
00:16:02,561 --> 00:16:04,560
Look how easy it springs across.

234
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:07,320
Works really great.

235
00:16:09,700 --> 00:16:14,040
Just three years after this wet mill was built in Kona in 1926,

236
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,080
the Probat factory in Germany manufactured this roaster

237
00:16:18,081 --> 00:16:21,440
that Mike Sheldrake, a cafe owner in Long Beach, California,

238
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:24,040
now uses every day to roast coffee.

239
00:16:25,020 --> 00:16:29,520
This is a 1929 vintage German coffee roasting machine.

240
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:34,020
This machine will do about 30 pounds of coffee per batch,

241
00:16:34,021 --> 00:16:36,460
and we can do about 100 pounds an hour.

242
00:16:37,180 --> 00:16:40,180
And routinely we'll roast 10,000 to 12,000 pounds a month.

243
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:43,340
Now when the coffee gets close to being done,

244
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,740
I'm going to keep sampling it with this trier

245
00:16:45,741 --> 00:16:48,220
and I'm going to look for bean development.

246
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:49,800
I'm going to look for size.

247
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:50,680
I'm going to look for color.

248
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:51,900
I'm going to look for bean pattern.

249
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:54,280
I'm going to listen for a particular sound.

250
00:16:54,281 --> 00:16:57,120
I want to get the coffee exactly the way I want it.

251
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:00,860
And this coffee is right there now.

252
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:03,640
I'm going to dump it on the cooling tray like this.

253
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:06,220
to draw all the heat out of the coffee,

254
00:17:06,221 --> 00:17:08,020
get it below 400 degrees.

255
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:15,960
That's as good as the coffee is ever gonna be.

256
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:20,340
Some of America's best known coffee brands

257
00:17:20,341 --> 00:17:21,980
started out much like this.

258
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:27,620
In San Francisco in 1881, two brothers,

259
00:17:27,621 --> 00:17:30,640
Austin and Rubin Hills, began roasting coffee beans

260
00:17:30,641 --> 00:17:32,640
imported from Central and South America

261
00:17:32,641 --> 00:17:34,880
in front of their store.

262
00:17:34,881 --> 00:17:39,080
The aroma attracted customers and sales took off.

263
00:17:39,081 --> 00:17:44,800
Three years later, the Hills Brothers abandoned retail sales for the wholesale coffee business.

264
00:17:44,801 --> 00:17:50,040
The familiar vacuum packed can, still used by most coffee companies today, was pioneered

265
00:17:50,041 --> 00:17:53,220
by the Hills Brothers in 1900.

266
00:17:53,221 --> 00:18:00,260
The cans made it possible to keep coffee fresh over much longer periods.

267
00:18:00,261 --> 00:18:02,980
It would take the rest of the industry a decade to catch up.

268
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:08,220
At the turn of the century, there were two other large San Francisco-based coffee companies

269
00:18:08,221 --> 00:18:12,220
battling Hills Brothers for supremacy in the West, MJB and Folgers.

270
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:18,140
Back in those days, one of the most popular ways to brew coffee was the vacuum pot.

271
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:20,780
And today, it's making a comeback.

272
00:18:20,781 --> 00:18:34,160
The lower bowl holds the water, which is connected to the upper bowl by a seal.

273
00:18:34,161 --> 00:18:39,940
And then the bottom bowl is heated, and the water will boil in the bottom bowl.

274
00:18:39,941 --> 00:18:45,280
The pressure will build up on top of the water and force the water up through the tube, through

275
00:18:45,281 --> 00:18:49,200
the filter, and into the coffee grounds where it will actually brew.

276
00:18:49,201 --> 00:18:54,580
it's through brewing will remove the heat from the bottom bowl, it will form a vacuum,

277
00:18:54,581 --> 00:18:59,020
and suck the coffee through the coffee filter into the bottom bowl, making an incredibly

278
00:18:59,021 --> 00:19:01,280
delicious cup of coffee.

279
00:19:01,281 --> 00:19:03,899
In the early 1900s, most of the eastern U.S.

280
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:06,340
was dominated by the huge Arbuckle Coffee

281
00:19:06,341 --> 00:19:11,760
Company, whose plant along the Brooklyn waterfront occupied 12 city blocks.

282
00:19:11,761 --> 00:19:17,780
In 1913, Arbuckle rolled out a new premium Christmas blend, U-Ban.

283
00:19:17,781 --> 00:19:21,580
The name was apparently a truncation of Yuletide Banquet.

284
00:19:21,581 --> 00:19:24,780
After a huge local advertising campaign,

285
00:19:24,781 --> 00:19:27,980
U-Ban became the top-selling packaged coffee in New York City.

286
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:33,280
Arbuckle and U-Ban were eventually taken over

287
00:19:33,281 --> 00:19:37,480
by another coffee giant, Maxwell House.

288
00:19:37,481 --> 00:19:40,820
Maxwell House is another great branding story.

289
00:19:40,821 --> 00:19:43,980
There was a famous hotel in Tennessee

290
00:19:43,981 --> 00:19:46,680
known as the Maxwell House, and the chef

291
00:19:46,681 --> 00:19:51,420
was so concerned about his coffee, they created his own blend, which became the Maxwell House

292
00:19:51,421 --> 00:19:52,420
blend.

293
00:19:52,421 --> 00:19:58,160
The growth of big coffee in the U.S. coincided with the growth of big advertising.

294
00:19:58,161 --> 00:20:03,500
Over the next few decades, national ad agencies based in New York City helped create national

295
00:20:03,501 --> 00:20:09,300
coffee giants that are still popular, including U-Ban, Hills Brothers, Holgers, and Maxwell

296
00:20:09,301 --> 00:20:10,300
House.

297
00:20:10,301 --> 00:20:12,460
How do you do, ladies and gentlemen?

298
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:15,460
The growth of broadcasting networks accelerated the process.

299
00:20:15,461 --> 00:20:19,360
This is Harry Bonzel introducing something different in radio entertainment.

300
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:25,540
And finally, about 75% of the total coffee volume in the United States

301
00:20:25,541 --> 00:20:28,600
was concentrated in the hands of just five companies.

302
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:33,440
For decades, advertising, broadcasting, and coffee

303
00:20:33,441 --> 00:20:36,860
fueled each other in a gigantic percolating money machine.

304
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:39,340
But the coffee wasn't getting any better,

305
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:41,820
and percolators, in fact, were part of the problem.

306
00:20:41,821 --> 00:20:49,980
In the 1950s, the percolator was hot, a symbol of modernity and industrial progress on your

307
00:20:49,981 --> 00:20:54,340
very own stovetop. It even made a pleasant bubbling sound as it worked.

308
00:20:57,880 --> 00:21:00,270
This is the coffee that our parents drank.

309
00:21:00,271 --> 00:21:03,400
There's no worse way that you could ever produce coffee

310
00:21:03,401 --> 00:21:05,600
or bastardize coffee than with a percolator.

311
00:21:05,601 --> 00:21:07,800
A percolator basically takes the grounds and

312
00:21:07,801 --> 00:21:10,300
continually runs it through over and over and over and over.

313
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:15,220
As the water percolates through the coffee, it falls back to the bottom of the pot.

314
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:21,000
There, it's reheated and forced back through the pumping system, over the coffee grounds again.

315
00:21:22,660 --> 00:21:25,380
Over-percolating can make the coffee undrinkable.

316
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:32,660
The two really bad things are reheating the coffee, because that changes it chemically,

317
00:21:32,661 --> 00:21:39,040
and then rebrewing that coffee through the coffee grounds.

318
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:41,900
To make matters worse, according to experts,

319
00:21:41,901 --> 00:21:46,160
the quality of some major coffee brands sold in supermarkets was declining.

320
00:21:47,420 --> 00:21:50,400
Experts say price competition drove some leading brands

321
00:21:50,401 --> 00:21:53,340
to put cheaper and cheaper grades of ground coffee in their cans.

322
00:21:54,960 --> 00:21:58,040
And it was after these businesses were sold

323
00:21:58,041 --> 00:22:00,920
and the families were no longer in control

324
00:22:00,921 --> 00:22:03,520
that then the accountants took over.

325
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:09,440
And the assumption was, well, if we save a few pennies on the blend cost,

326
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:14,880
it's millions of dollars on the bottom line, and the consumers won't notice.

327
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:21,060
Some well-known brands began adding more of the inexpensive Robusta coffee beans to their blends.

328
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:27,900
There are two types of beans that are commercially grown.

329
00:22:28,560 --> 00:22:30,400
One is Robusta and one is Arabica.

330
00:22:30,401 --> 00:22:36,540
The Robusta coffee is the low-grade quality coffee that is found in instant coffee

331
00:22:36,541 --> 00:22:42,040
and in many ways the commercial coffee that America's been drinking for the past 50 years.

332
00:22:42,640 --> 00:22:44,480
And Americans did not know any better.

333
00:22:45,380 --> 00:22:51,180
Widely grown in Vietnam and parts of Africa, Robusta plants resist disease and are easier to grow.

334
00:22:52,040 --> 00:22:55,160
They contain much more caffeine than the higher-priced Arabica beans,

335
00:22:55,840 --> 00:22:58,000
but produce a harsh, flat, and bitter brew.

336
00:22:58,001 --> 00:23:01,460
America was drinking swill.

337
00:23:04,100 --> 00:23:09,120
And I would like to say that Starbucks in many ways

338
00:23:09,121 --> 00:23:14,480
created a sense of awareness and a trial vehicle

339
00:23:14,481 --> 00:23:16,900
for people to recognize that there was something else.

340
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:21,900
Good quality or bad, millions of people in America and around the world

341
00:23:21,901 --> 00:23:23,440
need their morning cup of coffee.

342
00:23:24,060 --> 00:23:27,480
No matter how it tastes, much of coffee's popularity

343
00:23:27,481 --> 00:23:32,280
has to do with caffeine, a drug that stimulates the production of adrenal hormones.

344
00:23:33,080 --> 00:23:37,680
That causes a rise in blood sugar, producing the lift the coffee provides.

345
00:23:38,840 --> 00:23:42,840
Insulin is then secreted by the body, causing sugar levels to return to normal.

346
00:23:43,880 --> 00:23:46,800
Most health experts say moderate coffee consumption is safe,

347
00:23:47,420 --> 00:23:49,940
but excessive amounts could cause problems.

348
00:23:51,180 --> 00:23:54,700
Coffee's been one of the lubricants of the industrialized world,

349
00:23:54,701 --> 00:23:59,640
And it's really the role that caffeine plays in stimulating our central nervous system

350
00:23:59,641 --> 00:24:06,120
and making us speak more clearly, think more clearly, improves our motor skills.

351
00:24:06,540 --> 00:24:13,460
It really lends itself to all of the activities that we normally undertake as part of an industrialized society.

352
00:24:15,440 --> 00:24:20,840
The majority of coffee consumers still buy the lower-priced cans of ground coffee at the supermarket.

353
00:24:20,841 --> 00:24:26,080
But sales of so-called specialty or gourmet coffees are growing.

354
00:24:26,081 --> 00:24:31,300
And in dollar value, this represents about half of coffee sales in the U.S.

355
00:24:31,301 --> 00:24:37,960
In 1960, overall per capita consumption of coffee in America was 15.5 pounds annually.

356
00:24:37,961 --> 00:24:42,840
Today it's only 57% of that, less than 9 pounds annually.

357
00:24:42,841 --> 00:24:50,420
The reason that consumption declined in the United States is that the commercial blends

358
00:24:50,421 --> 00:24:56,420
began to undermine their quality by introducing more and more lower grades and particularly

359
00:24:56,421 --> 00:24:59,778
more and more robusta type coffees.

360
00:24:59,779 --> 00:25:04,480
And so the consumer very predictably responded by

361
00:25:04,481 --> 00:25:09,740
consuming less of the product. It would take the ultimate coffee making machine,

362
00:25:09,741 --> 00:25:14,940
the modern espresso machine, to create a new passion for great coffee.

363
00:25:14,941 --> 00:25:23,980
The largest retailer of cups of coffee in America is Dunkin' Donuts,

364
00:25:24,300 --> 00:25:28,520
which sells about a billion cups a year, or 30 cups every second.

365
00:25:31,940 --> 00:25:36,100
Here in the cupping department at Starbucks corporate headquarters in Seattle,

366
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:41,180
coffee tasters sample beans at various stages, from delivery to roasting to packaging.

367
00:25:41,181 --> 00:25:47,320
The cupping room is the nerve center really for any coffee company because it's where

368
00:25:47,321 --> 00:25:50,180
all the quality evaluations are made.

369
00:25:50,181 --> 00:25:52,160
Cupping is an art essentially.

370
00:25:52,161 --> 00:25:55,940
We take a certain amount of coffee and we roast it, grind it and put it into a glass.

371
00:25:55,941 --> 00:25:59,860
And then we take nearly boiling water and pour over the top of that.

372
00:25:59,861 --> 00:26:03,940
And we leave the grounds in there so you get all of the intensities, all of the aromas,

373
00:26:03,941 --> 00:26:08,700
all the flavors.

374
00:26:08,701 --> 00:26:12,460
On a busy day, the department tastes as many as 800 cups.

375
00:26:12,461 --> 00:26:15,300
If they drank all the coffee they sipped,

376
00:26:15,301 --> 00:26:17,940
they'd probably never get any sleep.

377
00:26:17,941 --> 00:26:20,620
But just as professional wine tasters do,

378
00:26:20,621 --> 00:26:22,980
cuppers spit out most of the liquid,

379
00:26:22,981 --> 00:26:24,660
creating coffee in much the same way

380
00:26:24,661 --> 00:26:26,060
as a glass of Chardonnay.

381
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:30,000
There's a lot of similarities.

382
00:26:30,001 --> 00:26:33,660
We use terms like buttery, nutty, caramelly,

383
00:26:33,661 --> 00:26:35,420
a lot of different things you would find

384
00:26:35,421 --> 00:26:36,820
both in wine and in coffee.

385
00:26:36,821 --> 00:26:37,820
I've got a lot of them.

386
00:26:37,821 --> 00:26:39,420
It's like a dark one. Yeah, it's very nice.

387
00:26:43,060 --> 00:26:46,680
Plenty of cupping goes on at the Specialty Coffee Association of America,

388
00:26:46,860 --> 00:26:48,320
based in Long Beach, California.

389
00:26:48,560 --> 00:26:50,520
It teaches people how to become tasting pros.

390
00:26:51,300 --> 00:26:56,440
We've taken what had been a tradition or sort of an art form that we call cupping

391
00:26:56,441 --> 00:27:01,820
and translated that into a very specific science.

392
00:27:02,940 --> 00:27:06,620
What's going on right now is 10 of some of the best cuppers in Colombia are here

393
00:27:06,621 --> 00:27:09,780
through a U.S. aid-funded government fund.

394
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:15,200
And they're here doing a training on how to use some of the standards that we use to assess quality.

395
00:27:17,400 --> 00:27:22,320
So that it covers the entire palate, the coffee is sipped vigorously and loudly.

396
00:27:23,400 --> 00:27:28,640
When you first get started with cupping, a lot of people end up kind of choking on some of the coffee,

397
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:30,280
but you get used to it after a while.

398
00:27:30,580 --> 00:27:32,340
Usually the louder it is, the better you are.

399
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:40,420
Although coffee experts have common reference points,

400
00:27:40,421 --> 00:27:42,400
when it comes to their favorite cup of coffee,

401
00:27:42,560 --> 00:27:43,820
opinions differ widely.

402
00:27:45,420 --> 00:27:47,660
For me, it's coffee from Indonesia.

403
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:51,580
Sumatra, Java, Silowese, New Guinea.

404
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:54,480
My favorite way would be a quadruple espresso,

405
00:27:55,040 --> 00:27:56,480
probably made with Brazilian espresso,

406
00:27:57,200 --> 00:27:58,680
being scented with cardamom.

407
00:27:58,740 --> 00:28:00,600
I don't want to drink a big, heavy Sumatra

408
00:28:00,601 --> 00:28:02,160
at 6 o'clock in the morning.

409
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:03,680
I like a lighter coffee.

410
00:28:03,720 --> 00:28:05,160
I like an East African coffee then.

411
00:28:05,161 --> 00:28:08,200
My favorite coffee is Ethiopian coffee, without a doubt.

412
00:28:08,201 --> 00:28:12,120
I tend to take a drip, use a drip machine to make coffee in the morning,

413
00:28:12,121 --> 00:28:13,960
and I always choose beans that are Colombian.

414
00:28:13,961 --> 00:28:17,560
I personally use the French press, the press method,

415
00:28:17,561 --> 00:28:21,720
which is kind of similar to what we call the old cardboard coffees.

416
00:28:21,721 --> 00:28:24,200
The method of preparation I prefer is the French press,

417
00:28:25,240 --> 00:28:27,160
because you get the intensity of the flavor.

418
00:28:29,480 --> 00:28:33,640
This is a French press coffee brewer, and it's very simple to operate.

419
00:28:33,641 --> 00:28:40,660
You just put your coffee grounds in the pot, and then you fill the pot with hot water.

420
00:28:40,740 --> 00:28:44,780
And you want your water to be as close to 200 degrees as possible to get full extraction.

421
00:28:45,940 --> 00:28:51,700
And then you just use this metal filter that's reusable, and you just press the grounds down to the bottom.

422
00:28:52,680 --> 00:29:01,300
And you create a fresh cup of coffee that has all the oils and all the sediment and all the flavor that was originally in the coffee bean.

423
00:29:01,301 --> 00:29:08,960
Undoubtedly, the most expensive way to make coffee is to use one of these $6,000 commercial espresso machines.

424
00:29:10,300 --> 00:29:22,140
It's very important when you extract this coffee that you see very, very, very, very thin strands of coffee coming out, often the size of strands of spaghetti.

425
00:29:22,840 --> 00:29:27,280
The color we're seeing right now is a very, very deep rust color, and that's the color we want to see.

426
00:29:27,281 --> 00:29:36,480
It wasn't until 1948 in Milan that the espresso machine as we know it was perfected.

427
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:43,840
Cafe owner and tinkerer Achille Gaggia came up with the secret, which involves the use of a heavy spring and a piston.

428
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:50,820
This combination forces hot water through finely ground coffee much more rapidly than steam pressure alone,

429
00:29:51,120 --> 00:29:54,220
which old-fashioned espresso makers relied on.

430
00:29:54,221 --> 00:30:01,000
The piston and spring produce a much faster, and experts say superior, extraction of the coffee's flavor.

431
00:30:01,980 --> 00:30:05,660
Some of the components on an espresso machine would be, first of all, the boiler.

432
00:30:06,320 --> 00:30:07,820
Inside the boiler is a heating element.

433
00:30:07,920 --> 00:30:11,040
The heating element will heat up the water, which will create steam pressure.

434
00:30:12,260 --> 00:30:14,640
Hot water in the boiler doesn't go into your cup.

435
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:19,080
Instead, it heats up fresh water by means of a heat exchanger.

436
00:30:19,081 --> 00:30:24,320
Perhaps the pinnacle of espresso machine art is the cappuccino,

437
00:30:24,700 --> 00:30:28,340
a combination of espresso coffee and steamed milk.

438
00:30:29,320 --> 00:30:31,020
An original Italian cappuccino,

439
00:30:31,300 --> 00:30:33,280
the way the Italians have been making cappuccino forever,

440
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:35,200
you're going to see the coffee in here.

441
00:30:35,400 --> 00:30:39,420
One thing that you don't want it to do is have bitter espresso on the bottom

442
00:30:39,421 --> 00:30:41,280
and dry white foam on top.

443
00:30:41,281 --> 00:30:53,960
When Achille Gaggia came up with his new improved machine in 1948, it led to an espresso cafe

444
00:30:53,961 --> 00:31:00,620
boom in Italy in the early 1950s, which soon spread to the rest of Europe.

445
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:08,640
In modified form, the espresso boom hit San Francisco's North Beach area, where beatniks,

446
00:31:08,641 --> 00:31:12,640
modern jazz and beat poetry came together in coffee houses.

447
00:31:12,641 --> 00:31:19,640
The coffee house as a center of intrigue became as vital and resonant in San Francisco in

448
00:31:19,641 --> 00:31:27,069
the late 1950s, early 1960s as it had been in London in the late 17th century.

449
00:31:27,070 --> 00:31:27,770
Coffee

450
00:31:27,771 --> 00:31:34,640
as a stimulant of the imagination, as a kind of a glow that people would get as they gathered

451
00:31:34,641 --> 00:31:40,320
gathered around a warm fireplace of the spirit that became the coffee pot.

452
00:31:40,321 --> 00:31:48,160
All of this naturally became a potent source of a movement that was quintessentially based

453
00:31:48,161 --> 00:31:49,160
on ideas.

454
00:31:49,161 --> 00:31:54,760
As espresso helped ignite a counterculture, on the other side of the divide, offices

455
00:31:54,761 --> 00:31:59,320
and factories kept noses to the grindstone with machines that brewed bottomless cups

456
00:31:59,321 --> 00:32:00,600
of cheap coffee.

457
00:32:00,601 --> 00:32:08,100
But as the pace of modern life sped up, brewing was just too slow for some Americans.

458
00:32:08,101 --> 00:32:10,360
They wanted their caffeine fix to be instant.

459
00:32:10,361 --> 00:32:13,120
And so it was.

460
00:32:13,121 --> 00:32:18,980
But to make what the industry calls insoluble coffee would require a lot more ingenuity

461
00:32:18,981 --> 00:32:25,800
and technology than it takes to roast a bean.

462
00:32:25,801 --> 00:32:30,800
One of the most expensive coffees in the world is Kopi Lua from Indonesia.

463
00:32:30,801 --> 00:32:36,980
It's made from coffee beans digested and excreted by the palm civet, a cat-like animal, and

464
00:32:36,981 --> 00:32:41,720
sells for around $300 a pound.

465
00:32:41,721 --> 00:32:44,590
Although instant coffee is less popular in the U.S.

466
00:32:44,591 --> 00:32:46,560
than it once was, in most of Europe

467
00:32:46,561 --> 00:32:49,800
and Asia, it's the number one way to make coffee.

468
00:32:49,801 --> 00:32:54,660
The UK, interestingly, is about 80% instant share,

469
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:57,600
only 20% roast and ground.

470
00:32:58,060 --> 00:33:00,040
Much of the third world, developing world,

471
00:33:00,400 --> 00:33:01,500
it's still instant coffee.

472
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:06,200
Worldwide, about 40% of the coffee market at retail

473
00:33:06,201 --> 00:33:07,840
is instant coffee,

474
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:11,180
and Nescafe dominates this part of the market.

475
00:33:11,640 --> 00:33:13,440
The development of instant coffee

476
00:33:13,441 --> 00:33:15,180
can be traced back to the Civil War,

477
00:33:15,580 --> 00:33:18,520
when soldiers on both sides craved coffee so much

478
00:33:18,521 --> 00:33:22,420
that some carried carbines with a coffee mill built into the buttstock.

479
00:33:23,740 --> 00:33:27,880
Eventually, some Union soldiers received an early crude form of instant coffee.

480
00:33:29,520 --> 00:33:32,140
Finely ground coffee was mixed with sugar and milk

481
00:33:32,141 --> 00:33:36,080
to form a concentrated gooey paste that was sealed in a can.

482
00:33:37,120 --> 00:33:39,660
By adding hot water to a spoonful of paste,

483
00:33:40,240 --> 00:33:43,120
a soldier could enjoy a fast and reasonably good cup of coffee.

484
00:33:45,140 --> 00:33:47,660
Instant coffee powder first came to America

485
00:33:47,661 --> 00:33:52,220
after an English chemist living in Guatemala named George Washington

486
00:33:52,221 --> 00:33:55,940
developed a method to refine coffee crystals from brewed coffee.

487
00:33:56,720 --> 00:33:59,600
He moved to New York City, became an American citizen,

488
00:34:00,180 --> 00:34:02,780
and began selling Washington's refined coffee.

489
00:34:04,180 --> 00:34:06,520
A dehydrated coffee was only a minor success,

490
00:34:07,120 --> 00:34:11,540
but when World War I broke out, the U.S. Army requisitioned his entire output.

491
00:34:11,541 --> 00:34:18,940
After World War I, instant coffee went back to being a marginal product, and it would

492
00:34:18,941 --> 00:34:24,860
take another World War to stir up interest.

493
00:34:24,861 --> 00:34:30,540
In World War II, GIs had instant coffee in their K-rations that came from 12 different

494
00:34:30,541 --> 00:34:37,100
suppliers, including George Washington, Maxwell House, and Nescafe.

495
00:34:37,101 --> 00:34:39,420
Hot coffee in the trenches meant a lot.

496
00:34:39,421 --> 00:34:42,380
It was a great comfort to war-weary soldiers,

497
00:34:42,381 --> 00:34:46,980
and instant coffee powder made it much faster and easier to make.

498
00:34:46,981 --> 00:34:51,180
Throughout World War II, the U.S. military actually was responsible for spreading

499
00:34:51,181 --> 00:34:55,460
Nescafe and instant coffee around the globe,

500
00:34:55,461 --> 00:35:00,920
making instant coffee very popular in markets across Europe and Asia.

501
00:35:00,921 --> 00:35:07,740
For decades, instant coffee was manufactured using the spray-dry process.

502
00:35:07,741 --> 00:35:11,806
coffee is sprayed from a high tower in a large hot air chamber.

503
00:35:11,807 --> 00:35:13,420
As the droplets fall, the

504
00:35:13,421 --> 00:35:18,060
remaining water evaporates, and dry crystals of coffee drop to the bottom of the chamber.

505
00:35:19,260 --> 00:35:25,260
One of the drawbacks of spray drying is that it uses heat, and heat is one of coffee's mortal

506
00:35:25,261 --> 00:35:25,961
enemies.

507
00:35:25,962 --> 00:35:30,820
Heat is not very friendly to the finer properties of coffee flavor and aroma.

508
00:35:31,340 --> 00:35:36,080
In the 1960s, Nestle came out with a new way to make instant coffee,

509
00:35:36,081 --> 00:35:37,960
using a freeze-dry process.

510
00:35:38,680 --> 00:35:40,540
The benefit of freeze-dried coffee

511
00:35:40,541 --> 00:35:43,040
is that it took heat out of the equation.

512
00:35:43,800 --> 00:35:46,700
Freshly brewed coffee is condensed into an extract

513
00:35:46,701 --> 00:35:48,280
and frozen in slabs.

514
00:35:49,040 --> 00:35:51,280
The slabs are then ground into small chunks

515
00:35:51,281 --> 00:35:52,820
and put in vacuum chambers.

516
00:35:54,100 --> 00:35:56,360
Moisture in the form of ice is then drawn off,

517
00:35:57,000 --> 00:35:58,840
leaving dry coffee crystals behind.

518
00:36:00,820 --> 00:36:04,400
The most difficult part in instant or soluble coffee

519
00:36:04,401 --> 00:36:06,260
is maintaining the aromatics.

520
00:36:06,840 --> 00:36:10,080
Companies have made great strides in doing this.

521
00:36:10,220 --> 00:36:13,140
Some of them will actually condense smoke

522
00:36:13,141 --> 00:36:16,080
that comes out of a coffee roaster into a liquid form

523
00:36:16,081 --> 00:36:19,320
and reintroduce that liquid into the powder

524
00:36:19,321 --> 00:36:21,380
before the jar is sealed.

525
00:36:22,380 --> 00:36:25,020
The majority of the users of instant coffee

526
00:36:25,021 --> 00:36:27,180
are probably over the age of 50.

527
00:36:27,540 --> 00:36:30,540
However, we are seeing pockets of younger folks

528
00:36:30,541 --> 00:36:32,260
using instant coffee today.

529
00:36:32,261 --> 00:36:40,220
And one of the benefits we're seeing amongst the younger folks is there's not so much of a negative instant coffee perception,

530
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:45,660
but really it's a blank slate, and these people just have not been communicated to.

531
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:49,780
So at Nestle, we are passionate about instant coffee.

532
00:36:49,920 --> 00:36:53,260
It's the only part of the coffee business that we participate in.

533
00:36:53,900 --> 00:36:58,540
Yes, you really did hear the words passionate and instant coffee in the same sentence.

534
00:36:59,440 --> 00:37:02,060
We have passion for instant coffee. Yes, we do.

535
00:37:02,260 --> 00:37:06,620
And as a sign of the times, instant coffee is now going gourmet.

536
00:37:06,621 --> 00:37:14,380
One of the most recent introductions that we're making is a 100% Colombian blend of

537
00:37:14,381 --> 00:37:16,340
instant coffee.

538
00:37:16,341 --> 00:37:20,580
Coffee connoisseurs know Colombian beans as the finest beans there are.

539
00:37:20,581 --> 00:37:28,080
For many coffee connoisseurs, the subject of decaf is even more of a sore spot than instant.

540
00:37:28,081 --> 00:37:32,260
In some specialty coffee houses, a cup of decaf is called a why bother.

541
00:37:33,260 --> 00:37:36,260
To me, talking about decaf in a coffee is like talking about nothing.

542
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:37,540
So what can you say?

543
00:37:39,120 --> 00:37:44,740
But for people who experience tremors, palpitations, or just plain irritability from drinking caffeine,

544
00:37:45,280 --> 00:37:46,660
decaf is the only choice.

545
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:52,660
It was invented by Ludwig Roselius, a German merchant from Bremen,

546
00:37:52,661 --> 00:37:56,940
who patented his invention and started a company to produce it in 1906.

547
00:37:58,080 --> 00:38:03,500
Roselius believed that his father, a professional coffee taster, had died from consuming too

548
00:38:03,501 --> 00:38:04,614
much caffeine.

549
00:38:04,615 --> 00:38:10,900
Ludwig's new process extracted caffeine from green coffee beans by superheating

550
00:38:10,901 --> 00:38:16,842
them with steam and then flooding the softened beans with the solvent, benzol.

551
00:38:16,843 --> 00:38:17,680
The solvent

552
00:38:17,681 --> 00:38:22,309
was removed by rinsing the beans in water and steaming them.

553
00:38:22,310 --> 00:38:24,160
The caffeine-free coffee

554
00:38:24,161 --> 00:38:27,520
was soon being sold in Germany, France, and the U.S.

555
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:31,640
In France, it was given a name meaning sans caffeine.

556
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:35,200
Sanka.

557
00:38:36,400 --> 00:38:38,860
That eventually became its name everywhere.

558
00:38:39,880 --> 00:38:44,040
Drink five, ten cups a day, as many as you like,

559
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:46,820
and still stay steady as a rock.

560
00:38:47,220 --> 00:38:49,160
Familiar orange-colored Sanka jars

561
00:38:49,161 --> 00:38:51,240
are still found on supermarket shelves.

562
00:38:51,241 --> 00:38:58,260
In many offices and restaurants, orange-rimmed coffee pots are used to indicate decaf.

563
00:38:58,920 --> 00:39:04,720
The practice began decades ago, after Senka distributed thousands of orange-rimmed pots to restaurants.

564
00:39:06,380 --> 00:39:13,300
Some decaf coffees are now processed with water alone to remove the caffeine, while others still rely on solvents.

565
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:19,500
Instant and decaf both used science and technology to provide new ways to drink coffee.

566
00:39:19,501 --> 00:39:26,500
Decades later, an orthopedic surgeon in California would come up with a new way to grow coffee.

567
00:39:28,740 --> 00:39:33,960
More money is spent trading coffee than any other commodity in the world, except for oil.

568
00:39:36,980 --> 00:39:43,060
Although coffee is a beverage that's been around for centuries, there's always room for improvement.

569
00:39:43,061 --> 00:39:49,397
New directions are being explored constantly, and one of the explorers is Dr.

570
00:39:49,398 --> 00:39:50,220
Joe Albin,

571
00:39:50,700 --> 00:39:57,260
a.k.a. Kona Joe. He's an orthopedic surgeon practicing in Los Alamitos, California,

572
00:39:57,480 --> 00:39:59,719
south of Los Angeles. Dr.

573
00:39:59,720 --> 00:40:05,900
Albin leads a double life because he also owns and operates a 20-acre

574
00:40:05,901 --> 00:40:11,640
coffee farm in Kona, where he's developed a new way to grow coffee called the trellis method.

575
00:40:13,060 --> 00:40:16,640
No one has ever grown coffee before using a trellis.

576
00:40:16,680 --> 00:40:21,240
So this is a brand new idea that we thought of, and we patented it.

577
00:40:21,880 --> 00:40:25,060
If Dr. Alban's farm looks like a vineyard, it's with good reason.

578
00:40:26,240 --> 00:40:30,140
He developed the trellis method after studying the way wine grapes are grown.

579
00:40:31,420 --> 00:40:34,740
Not only is coffee a fruit, and wine grapes are a fruit,

580
00:40:34,741 --> 00:40:38,120
but many other fruits have been successfully trellised,

581
00:40:38,540 --> 00:40:42,660
like the Fuji apples, peaches, pears, and kiwis.

582
00:40:42,661 --> 00:40:47,340
And the trellis has increased production of all of those fruits,

583
00:40:47,460 --> 00:40:50,720
but also increased the sugars and made them all sweeter tasting.

584
00:40:51,540 --> 00:40:55,860
The trellis system allows more sunlight and air to reach the entire coffee plant.

585
00:40:57,560 --> 00:41:03,280
This is a tree that's now two years old, and it's just at the right age to be trained to the trellis.

586
00:41:03,500 --> 00:41:08,520
By laying this vertical on its side, we're stretching the plant, making it wider,

587
00:41:08,521 --> 00:41:11,520
and a vertical can give rise to laterals,

588
00:41:11,521 --> 00:41:13,520
but also new verticals.

589
00:41:15,520 --> 00:41:17,520
It was a long-term gamble,

590
00:41:17,521 --> 00:41:21,520
but the trellis method turned out to be a great success.

591
00:41:21,521 --> 00:41:23,520
These are our oldest, most mature trees.

592
00:41:23,521 --> 00:41:25,520
They're eight years old now,

593
00:41:25,521 --> 00:41:30,520
and we can see how that early split of the tree trunk,

594
00:41:30,521 --> 00:41:33,520
where we took those two flexible verticals,

595
00:41:33,521 --> 00:41:41,140
verticals has now turned into a very thick, hardy trunk that has this sort of T-shape.

596
00:41:42,340 --> 00:41:47,920
Kona Joe's trellis-grown coffee now sells at a premium price, and he can barely keep

597
00:41:47,921 --> 00:41:48,680
up with the demand.

598
00:41:50,520 --> 00:41:55,280
Each month, after two weeks on his coffee farm, Kona Joe becomes Dr. Joe Alban again

599
00:41:55,281 --> 00:41:58,860
and returns to his medical practice in California.

600
00:42:00,020 --> 00:42:02,340
It is culture shock going back and forth.

601
00:42:02,341 --> 00:42:08,140
When I go back to California now, I take off my blue jeans and Aloha shirt, scrub my hands,

602
00:42:08,540 --> 00:42:12,160
put on my white jacket, and go back to face some very serious problems.

603
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:17,640
And it's always an adjustment for me to go back and forth between the roles.

604
00:42:18,740 --> 00:42:22,980
Coffee growers in Kona have a much higher standard of living than most of the world's

605
00:42:22,981 --> 00:42:26,620
coffee farmers, many of whom barely manage to survive.

606
00:42:26,621 --> 00:42:33,220
The fair trade movement tries to address that by providing fair trade certification when

607
00:42:33,221 --> 00:42:39,980
coffee buyers pay a minimum of $1.26 per pound to small independent growers.

608
00:42:39,981 --> 00:42:44,180
Fair trade coffee sells well in Europe, where the movement began, and sales are growing

609
00:42:44,181 --> 00:42:46,460
in America.

610
00:42:46,461 --> 00:42:50,700
Yet another new direction for coffee is genetic engineering.

611
00:42:50,701 --> 00:42:55,060
Although it's still at the experimental stage, some speculate that scientists will eventually

612
00:42:55,061 --> 00:42:59,410
develop coffee trees that efficiently produce decaffeinated beans.

613
00:42:59,411 --> 00:43:00,860
But so far, there's no

614
00:43:00,861 --> 00:43:02,765
talk of a cappuccino tree.

615
00:43:02,766 --> 00:43:07,160
Probably in the near term, the genetic engineering that will

616
00:43:07,161 --> 00:43:12,520
have the greatest impact will be in the fruit ripening, particularly for coffees coming

617
00:43:12,521 --> 00:43:18,100
out of Brazil, where so much of the coffee is now mechanically harvested. Being able

618
00:43:18,101 --> 00:43:23,300
to have all of the fruit ripen at the same time is very important to the efficiency of

619
00:43:23,301 --> 00:43:28,004
that process. The long-term future of coffee looks good.

620
00:43:28,005 --> 00:43:30,860
As high-quality coffee consumption

621
00:43:30,861 --> 00:43:36,660
increases and drinkers worldwide turn to their regular cups for more than just a daily jolt.

622
00:43:37,340 --> 00:43:44,683
My forecast would be that in the next hundred years coffee will double in its volume.

623
00:43:44,684 --> 00:43:45,460
What will

624
00:43:45,461 --> 00:43:51,460
much different is that coffee will truly move into the wine model,

625
00:43:51,461 --> 00:43:55,460
and it will become less and less a commodity.

626
00:43:55,461 --> 00:44:00,460
No doubt coffee will still play a vital role that no fine wine can ever match,

627
00:44:00,461 --> 00:44:05,460
as long as it continues to get most of the world going every morning.

